::PC_U04_L04_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U04_L04_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.4
Question:
Which formula is correct for sin(A+B)?
{
~A. sin(A)+sin(B)#Sine does not distribute over addition.
~B. sin(A)sin(B)+cos(A)cos(B)#This is connected to cosine difference, not sine sum.
=C. sin(A)cos(B)+cos(A)sin(B)#Correct. The sine sum formula is sin(A)cos(B)+cos(A)sin(B).
~D. cos(A)sin(B)-sin(A)cos(B)#This has the wrong structure and sign for sine sum.
}
::PC_U04_L04_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U04_L04_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.4
Question:
Which formula can be used for cos(2x)?
{
=A. cos^2(x)-sin^2(x)#Correct. One double-angle form is cos(2x)=cos^2(x)-sin^2(x).
~B. sin^2(x)+cos^2(x)#This is the Pythagorean identity, not a double-angle formula.
~C. 2sin(x)cos(x)#This is the double-angle formula for sine.
~D. tan(x)/(1-tan^2(x))#This is a tangent double-angle structure, not cosine.
}
::PC_U04_L04_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U04_L04_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.4
Question:
Why does a half-angle formula require sign attention?
{
~A. Half-angle formulas never use signs#Half-angle formulas include a positive or negative square-root choice.
~B. The answer is always positive#Some half-angle values are positive, but not all.
~C. The answer is always negative#Some half-angle values are negative, but not all.
=D. The quadrant of the half-angle determines the sign#Correct. The sign depends on the quadrant where the half-angle lies.
}
::PC_U04_L04_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U04_L04_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.4
Question:
What is sin(2x) when sin(x)=3/5 and cos(x)=4/5?
{
~A. 7/5#This adds sine and cosine instead of using the formula.
=B. 24/25#Correct. sin(2x)=2sin(x)cos(x)=2(3/5)(4/5)=24/25.
~C. 12/25#This leaves out the factor of 2.
~D. 1/5#This does not use the sine double-angle formula.
}
::PC_U04_L04_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U04_L04_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.4
Question:
Which statement is a common formula mistake?
{
~A. cos(A-B)=cos(A)cos(B)+sin(A)sin(B)#This is the correct cosine difference formula.
~B. sin(2x)=2sin(x)cos(x)#This is the correct sine double-angle formula.
=C. sin(A+B)=sin(A)+sin(B)#Correct. Sine of a sum is not the sum of the sines.
~D. tan(A+B)=(tan(A)+tan(B))/(1-tan(A)tan(B))#This is the correct tangent sum formula when defined.
}